<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/metaphysics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Metaphysics asks <strong>what fundamentally exists, and what it is like at the deepest
    level</strong> — the inventory of reality. The name comes from the accident of
    library-shelving ("the books <em>after</em> the Physics"), but the questions are not
    accidental at all: is a number a thing? Does your childhood exist, or only memories of
    it? Are you the same person as the child in the photograph? These are not mysticism;
    they are rigorous arguments about ontological bookkeeping — and their answers quietly
    underwrite physics, mathematics, law and every other discipline's implicit picture of
    what the world contains. The syllabus files this lesson as the "atlas extension": after
    the core (argument, knowledge), it surveys the wider territory — objects, properties,
    time and possibility, plus the neighbouring provinces of political philosophy,
    philosophy of science, Eastern philosophy and aesthetics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classic questions — existence, universals, time, possibility" widget="equationStep" config={metaCfg} />

  <h2>Objects and properties: universals, and the ship that wouldn't stay the same</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest metaphysical problem is <strong>the problem of universals</strong>. Two red
    apples share something — redness — and science talks of nothing else: electrons share
    charge, atoms share structure. Does the shared property <em>exist</em>, and where?
    <strong>Realists</strong> say yes: Plato located universals in a timeless realm of
    Forms (particular red apples "participate" in Redness itself); Aristotle relocated
    them into the things themselves (no redness without red things). <strong>Nominalists</strong>
    say only particulars exist — "red" is a name we apply, a resemblance, a set-membership
    — and the history of metaphysics is largely the ping-pong between these camps, with
    mathematics as the hard case: numbers behave exactly like realist universals
    (objective, necessary, shared by all their instances) while never being found in any
    experiment. <strong>Platonism about mathematics</strong> (numbers really exist,
    non-physically) versus <strong>nominalism</strong> (mathematics is an indispensable
    fiction) remains genuinely open — and it matters, because "mathematics is discovered
    or invented?" is this question wearing casual clothes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Objects</strong> raise the sibling problem: identity through change. The
    <strong>Ship of Theseus</strong> — planks replaced one by one until none remain; the
    old planks then reassembled into a second ship — asks which is the original, and the
    honest answer is that persistence through change is genuinely unresolved. The
    positions on offer: <strong>spatiotemporal continuity</strong> (the repaired ship
    stayed put in one continuous history — it is the same), <strong>psychological or
    biological continuity</strong> (for people: memory chains, the living body),
    <strong>four-dimensionalism</strong> (objects are processes in spacetime —
    "stage-worms" — and "same ship" is a relation between stages, not an all-or-nothing
    fact), or <strong>conventionalism</strong> (identity over time is partly our
    stipulation, and the puzzle shows the concept's edge). Applied everywhere: personal
    identity (the philosophy-of-mind lesson), legal personhood (corporations, AIs?), and
    biological individuality (colonial organisms, half-identical twins).
  </p>

  <h2>Time: does the future exist?</h2>
  <p>
    Common sense says the present is special: the past <em>was</em>, the future
    <em>will be</em>, only now <em>is</em>. That is <strong>presentism</strong> — only the
    present is real. Its rival, <strong>eternalism</strong>, says past, present and
    future all exist, spread out like a landscape — the "block universe." The modern
    argument tilts physical: relativity's <em>relativity of simultaneity</em> means there
    is no universal "now" — two observers moving relative to each other disagree about
    which distant events are happening "at the same time" — which undermines the
    metaphysics of a single objective present, and pushed many philosophers (and
    physicists such as Einstein himself, in a famous condolence letter: "the distinction
    between past, present and future is only a stubbornly persistent illusion") toward
    eternalism. Presentists respond that physics describes structure, not ontology, and
    that eternalism makes the future's openness — and with it free will and becoming —
    hard to state. Note the texture of the debate: neither side disputes any
    <em>experiment</em>; they dispute what the experiments imply about what exists. That
    is metaphysics functioning normally.
  </p>

  <h2>Possibility: possible worlds and modality</h2>
  <p>
    Everyday reasoning is saturated with modality — <em>could</em>, <em>must</em>,
    <em>might have been</em> — and metaphysics asks what makes such claims true or false.
    David Lewis's answer (1986) is the boldest: <strong>modal realism</strong> —
    possibilities are other <em>concrete</em> worlds, as real as ours, spatiotemporally
    isolated from it; "the coin could have landed tails" means: in some world, it did.
    The elegance (modality becomes quantification over worlds; modal logic gets clean
    truth-conditions) trades against ontology: an infinity of entire universes, most of
    them not containing you, existing for the sake of semantics. Alternatives:
    <strong>actualism</strong> — possibilities are ways <em>this</em> world could have
    been (abstract objects, or ersatz worlds built from propositions); <strong>fictionalism</strong>
    — possible worlds are a useful make-believe. Where you land shapes modal logic,
    counterfactual explanations in science, and how seriously to take the many-worlds
    interpretation of quantum mechanics — a physics proposal that, unusually, would make
    Lewis's ontology empirical.
  </p>

  <h2>Political philosophy: the social contract</h2>
  <p>
    The atlas's first extension asks what justifies the state. <strong>Social contract
    theory</strong> answers by construction: imagine humans with no government and ask
    what they would rationally agree to. <strong>Hobbes</strong> (1651): the state of
    nature is a "war of all against all" where life is "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish,
    and short" — so self-preservation licenses surrendering nearly all freedom to an
    absolute sovereign; order first, liberty later (his argument was written into the
    era's absolutism, and against it). <strong>Locke</strong> (1689): the state of nature
    already has natural rights — life, liberty, property — and government is a limited
    trustee that we may <em>replace</em> when it betrays the trust; the intellectual
    blueprint of constitutional democracy and the American founding.
    <strong>Rousseau</strong> (1762): the contract must unite citizens under the
    "general will" — laws we give ourselves collectively — freedom as self-rule rather
    than as the absence of constraint; the fuel, fair or not, of the revolutions that
    followed. Two centuries of refinement sharpened the game: Rawls (1971) re-ran the
    contract behind a "veil of ignorance" (design society not knowing who you will be in
    it) and derived liberal-egalitarian principles; Nozick ran it libertarian; critics
    (Hume first: no one ever <em>signed</em>) press on consent's fiction. The
    underwriting, though, is real: every constitution is an answer to the contract's
    question.
  </p>

  <h2>Philosophy of science: induction, demarcation and Kuhn</h2>
  <p>
    The second extension asks what makes science knowledge. <strong>The problem of
    induction</strong> (Hume, 1739): no number of confirming observations — however many
    white swans — logically guarantees the next one; science's basic inference is not
    provable without circularity. Responses define the 20th century.
    <strong>Popper's demarcation:</strong> science is distinguished not by
    <em>proving</em> theories but by making them <em>falsifiable</em> — a theory that
    cannot possibly fail is not science (a criterion aimed, polemically, at Freud and
    Marx as much as at astrology); science proceeds by bold conjecture and ruthless
    test. <strong>Kuhn's revolutions</strong> (1962): actual science runs in
    "paradigms" — accepted exemplars and methods — that accumulate anomalies until a
    crisis replaces the whole framework ("normal science" between revolutions); the
    image of science as steady falsification, Kuhn showed, fits the textbook, not the
    history. Popper and Kuhn still frame every argument about pseudoscience, replication
    and expert trust — the philosophy-of-science ladder lesson takes them up in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Eastern philosophy: Vedanta to Zen</h2>
  <p>
    The atlas is wider than the Western canon. <strong>Vedanta</strong> (the closing
    schools of the Vedas) centres on <em>Brahman</em> — ultimate reality — and the
    identity of <em>Atman</em>, the innermost self, with it: Shankara's Advaita ("not
    two") argues the everyday world of separate selves is <em>maya</em>, appearance; a
    metaphysical thesis argued with the same rigour (and some of the same puzzles) as
    Western idealism. <strong>Buddhism</strong> builds a metaphysics of
    <em>anatta</em> (no permanent self — persons are causally connected processes, the
    flame passed candle to candle) and <em>pratītyasamutpāda</em> (dependent origination:
    everything arises conditioned) — arguments that anticipate four-dimensionalism and
    process philosophy by two millennia, attached to a therapeutic method:
    meditation as investigation. <strong>Zen</strong> (Chan, from China's Chan Buddhism
    to Japan) distrusts conceptual elaboration itself — koans ("what is the sound of one
    hand?") are designed to break discursive thought and force direct insight — a live
    philosophical position about the <em>limits</em> of the argument method this unit
    teaches, from inside the tradition. Together with Confucian ethics (role, ritual,
    relationship) and Daoist naturalism (the <em>Dao</em> that "spoken, is not the
    constant Dao"), they make the point the atlas exists to make: the deepest questions
    have multiple working traditions, and reading only one is a smaller map.
  </p>

  <h2>Aesthetics: beauty, taste and the art-argument</h2>
  <p>
    The last extension asks whether beauty is in the object or the eye.
    <strong>Objectivists</strong> (Plato: beauty as a Form; the classical canon of
    proportion) say some things are beautiful simpliciter;
    <strong>subjectivists</strong> say taste decides — and Hume gave subjectivism its
    best form: beauty is a sentiment, but <em>judges of taste</em> can be trained, so
    verdicts are not equal ("a young man whose passions are warm" — the standard). Kant
    split the difference in the <em>Critique of Judgment</em>: judgements of beauty
    claim universal agreement ("this <em>is</em> beautiful", not "I like it") yet rest
    on no concept — the "free play" of the faculties, disinterested pleasure. The
    modern <strong>art-argument</strong> (Danto, Dickie): after Duchamp's urinal
    (1917) — an ordinary object, declared art by context and theory — art cannot be
    defined by beautiful appearance at all; it is whatever the <em>artworld</em>
    confers the status on (the institutional theory), or objects embodying an
    interpretation. The philosophy keeps its edge wherever practice outruns the
    definition: AI-generated images, found sound, process art — each a test case the
    objectivists, subjectivists and institutionalists grade differently.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer of metaphysics is its <strong>method</strong>: without experiments,
    the field works by systematic inference to the best explanation over our total
    commitments (Quine's "web of belief"), conceptual engineering (sharpening or
    revising the concepts themselves), and controlled intuition-pumping through
    thought experiments (the ship, the teleporter, the twin earth). Physics
    increasingly sets boundary conditions: relativity constrains time metaphysics;
    quantum mechanics revives questions about what makes an outcome actual (the
    measurement problem <em>is</em> a metaphysical question with laboratory stakes);
    thermodynamics grounds the arrow of time in entropy. Ontology's hardest open case
    remains mathematics (quoted in the widget): Benacerraf's dilemma — if numbers
    exist outside space and time, how do we know them; if they don't, why is
    mathematics objectively true? — has no consensus answer after fifty years.
  </p>
  <p>
    The atlas's four extensions share a spine worth naming: each asks where a
    practice's authority comes from — the state's (contract), science's (method),
    the tradition's (East and West), the artworld's (institutions) — and each finds
    the answer in something other than raw force or raw fact: agreement, testability,
    insight, conferred status. Metaphysics, the weirdest-looking subject, turns out to
    be the one that audits the others' hidden furniture — and the inventory is still
    open: numbers, possible worlds, the present moment, the self. Whether those exist,
    and in what sense, are live questions argued in journals this year; the widget's
    four questions are not history, they are the current frontier's front door.
  </p>
